Almost everything the CLI does is ultimately just creating, updating, or deleting Skupper CRDs.
When you run a CLI command, it:

Constructs the appropriate Custom Resource (Site, Listener, Connector, AccessGrant, etc.).
Applies it to the cluster (or local system).
Often waits for the resource to become ready and shows you status.

The CLI bascially creates, updates, and deletes CRDs.
CLI commands:
     1. construct a Custom Resource  (a site, listener, etc)
     2. Applies it to the cluster (or local system).
     3. waits for the resource to become ready and shows you status.


Look at some examples:

  CLI Command,                                What it does 
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  skupper site create west,                   Creates a Site resource named west
  skupper listener create backend 8080,       Creates a Listener resource
  skupper connector create backend 8080,      Creates a Connector resource
  skupper token issue / skupper link create,  Works with AccessGrant + AccessToken 
                                              (or creates a Link)
  skupper site status,                        Reads the status of the Site 
                                              (and related resources)


The CLI makes the common cases easy, while still allowing you to drop down 
to raw YAML when you need more control.


CLI commands allow you to generate YAML. instead of carrying out the command.
Like this:

     skupper listener create backend 8080 --generate

This way you can look at it, change it, then kubectl appy it.


